e see today so in a  certain sense the randomly fluctuating  universe idea of the multiverse  is actually more likely to have us start  with a high entropy condition  and we know that the entropy of the  universe if inflation took place has to  be extremely low in order to then  inflate and expand via the ordinary  expansion of the universe during  radiation domination etc  and even today to lambda domination and  into the future as what's called the  sitter space which is again an  exponentially accelerating universe  so to get to a 10 to 120th as your  entropy you could start with this very  very low value but you're almost more  likely according to the laws of  thermodynamics to start in high entropy  state rather than a low entropy state so  that's an extremely puzzling observation  now inflation as we've said doesn't  explain  how the universe got started it does  explain how the universe came to be at  least if inflation took place  geometrically flat  but on the other case it does  exceptionally successfully predict the  formation of seeds of structure if it  took place  and it also can create uh via the  so-called reheating mechanism it can  create the matter particles progenitors  that were then later fused into the  earliest nuclei in the universe that  appear in the  formation of big bang nuclear synthesis  so inflation is interesting it solves  many problems but it actually may make  the problem of the low entropy start of  the universe even harder to grapple with  so it could be that the universe had a  boundary in time if you extrapolate from  today back to  some beginning there would be a  beginning it wouldn't go through  smoothly to another universe  or it could be the so-called past  hypothesis is that there is low entropy  in this extremely early universe it  could be that the universe is eternal  and cyclic in a certain sense we don't  know what that means the multiverse  could play a role in its eternality  that inflation is everywhere and always  occurring not as a monetary phenomenon  but it is occurring at all points in the  cosmic superset of all points in space  and time today for the establishment of  the low entropy universe we must then  say that one of the explanations would  be an eternal universe that's always had  always had low entropy until at one  moment it decided to start inflating and  that is a paradigm that we could take  seriously we may believe that by  extrapolating back  the so-called board guth velenken  scenario does have a boundary the  question is is that a signature of when  classical general relativity breaks down  or is it a mandate that we have to then  come up with a theory of quantum gravity  we just don't know but these are  fascinating topics to discuss and  there's a third solution if the universe  was cyclical oscillating into and out of  existence maybe once maybe an infinite  number of times i always say what  happened 15 minutes before the big bang  hawking said that was as meaningless as  asking what's north of the north pole  and in contra distinction he put forth  the no boundary proposal that we talked  about many times  so is there anything before the big bang  it could be a singularity if you can  blindly extrapolate general relativity  back to the infinite past it could be  that there is some sort of phenomenon  that has caused the universe to exist  for eternity either via the so-called  conformal cyclic cosmology of roger  penrose or the so-called bouncing  cosmological models which neither of  which require  a quantum theory of gravity to hold true  and as you know i talk about quite often  the only two regimes where we think  about singularities as being necessary  or at the singularity of a black hole's  core or in the early universe but  neither of which can be possibly  observed even in principle in the  concept of the eternal inflationary  scenario we have a problem that is  solved conventionally via this infinite  number of universes popularized by the  lincoln and linden  cyclic models impose low entropy at the  beginning when the crunch occurs it  doesn't have to be a crunch it could be  a cycle it could be a bounce it could be  a classical bounce and actually the  amount of entropy need be only factors  of a few not exponential not  singularities in the new models of paul  steinhardt and aegis and others so these  are very interesting uh to think about  how it can behave to evade the previous  objections to say the model of tommy  gold and others of a cyclic universe in  which you would actually collapse so  much that you'd start the next cycle  with even higher entropy and add  infinitum the more cycles you add it on  eventually building up infinite entropy  each beginning so  this takes us back to evidence for or  against the multiverse and for or  against cyclic cosmologies i've talked  about this in previous videos that we  could actually observe perhaps the  imprint of a next-door universe via  these patterns in the cosmic microwave  background see here for a video  discussion of that when we talk about  universes beta universes i often like  this analogy of the petri dish with  colonies of bacteria embedded on it and  that the existence of a single bacterium  and its culture could then be used to  postulate that there is a petri dish and  here the analogy is the petri dish is  like the super space of the multiverse  and each culture each individual  bacteriological colony has its own  sort of connection or its own observable  universe in which it can communicate and  not come into contact with another  universe now when it does come into  contact there can be actual  bacteriological battles as i talked  about in losing the nobel prize so this  question can be observed perhaps if  inflation took place it can be  detectable perhaps with the simon's  observatory and the simon's array we'll  talk more about those but the logic that  we employ is that if we observe b mode  polarization we can infer from that a  background stochastic background of  gravitational waves which themselves can  only be created in quantized increments  that would then demonstrate the  quantization of gravity and that would  be further evidence for inflation and as  we let off with guth and linday saying  that in almost every model of inflation  you cannot escape the multiverse in  other words the logic is multiverse  produces inflation produces  gravitational waves produces b modes we  detect those b modes with our  cosmological telescopes the virtue of  the  non-inflationary universes the cyclic  models of siraja penrose and paul  steinhardt collaborators is that they do  not require the observation of  gravitational waves to validate in other  words if you observe a gravitational  wave polarization in the cmb of this  curl mode or [ __ ] type you will actually  be  falsifying the cosmological models of  the eon and the  bouncing or cyclic cosmological models  that is a virtue  and  i like to say we don't know yet what the  future will bring but in the words of  the namesake of the arthur c clarke  center for human imagination sir arthur  c clarke he said the only thing we can  be sure of the future is that it will be  absolutely fantastic  so for now this is brian keating  co-director of the arthur c clarke  center for human imagination at uc san  diego  thanking you for coming along on what we  call 10-minute thesis see you next time  if you like this video be sure to click  here to check out this video about the  inflationary multiverse and its  consequences if you'd like to learn more  about black holes and entropy watch this  video with juan mata cena don't forget  to subscribe
 

